dt_interface_client.c contains the following lines:

    if ((desiredPathForInterface = STRING_construct_sprintf("%s%s", DT_INTERFACE_PREFIX, dtInterfaceClient->componentName)) == NULL)
    {   
        LogError("Cannot build desiredPathForInterface string");
    }
    else if ((reportedPathForInterface = STRING_construct_sprintf("%s%s", DT_INTERFACE_PREFIX, dtInterfaceClient->componentName)) == NULL)
    {
        LogError("Cannot build reportedPathForInterface string");
    }

The call that constructs desiredPathForInterface is identical to the call that constructs reportedPathForInterface.  This should really just be a single call and a single variable, perhaps named pathForInterface.

No functional impact, but an unnecessary duplicate call, including memory allocation.

In the file `prov_transport_amqp_common.c`, we have the function `on_message_receiver_state_changed_callback`

```c
    switch (amqp_info->msg_recv_state)
    {
    case MESSAGE_RECEIVER_STATE_IDLE:
    case MESSAGE_RECEIVER_STATE_OPENING:
    case MESSAGE_RECEIVER_STATE_CLOSING:
        break;
    case MESSAGE_RECEIVER_STATE_OPEN:
        if (amqp_info->msg_send_state == MESSAGE_SENDER_STATE_OPEN)
        // removing for clarity...
        break;
    case MESSAGE_RECEIVER_STATE_ERROR:
        // removing for clarity...
        break;
    }
```

Note that there's no `default` here.  That was fine before as we covered all the cases, but some macro utils change I believe introduced an implicit `MESSAGE_SENDER_STATE_INVALID` type.  

Because this is not handled in the case statement and there is no default, clang on at least some configuration setting complained.

