body:
# Environment:

OS: Debian 9.
SDK: 2018-04-13 

# Description of the issue:

The issue occurs when calling the IoTHubRegistryManager_GetDevice function with a device ID that does not exist in the IoT Hub.

I would expect it to return the value IOTHUB_REGISTRYMANAGER_DEVICE_NOT_EXIST, but what it actually returns is IOTHUB_REGISTRYMANAGER_HTTP_STATUS_ERROR .

What I want is a "create if not exists" scenario, I was hoping to use the GetDevice function to identify when a device does not already exist and then use CreateDevice to create the device.  It looks like the SDK should support this, so I was surprised by the result returned by GetDevice when the device doesn't exist -- is this intentional or a bug?  Is there a better way to do a "create if not exists" using the SDK?

Calling the GetDevice function with a device ID for a device that does not exist also results in Errors being logged, which is not desirable for this use case.

# Code sample exhibiting the issue:

IOTHUB_SERVICE_CLIENT_AUTH_HANDLE serviceHandle = IoTHubServiceClientAuth_CreateFromConnectionString ("...");
IOTHUB_REGISTRYMANAGER_HANDLE registryHandle = IoTHubRegistryManager_Create (serviceHandle);
IOTHUB_DEVICE device;
IOTHUB_REGISTRYMANAGER_RESULT result;
result = IoTHubRegistryManager_GetDevice (registryHandle, "new-device", &device);

# Console log of the issue:

Error: Time:Fri May  4 08:55:49 2018 File:/src/azure-iot-sdk-c/c-utility/adapters/httpapi_curl.c Func:HTTPAPI_ExecuteRequest Line:624 Failure in HTTP communication: server reply code is 404
Info: HTTP Response:{"Message":"ErrorCode:DeviceNotFound;new-device","ExceptionMessage":"Tracking ID:622cd16543c24cad8152861964d8f6d9-G:4-TimeStamp:05/04/2018 08:55:49"}
Error: Time:Fri May  4 08:55:49 2018 File:/src/azure-iot-sdk-c/iothub_service_client/src/iothub_registrymanager.c Func:sendHttpRequestCRUD Line:999 Http Failure status code 404

I'm using Windows 10 and Visual Studio 2017.

I'm stepping through this tutorial: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/iot-accelerators/iot-accelerators-connecting-devices
I've added the following Nuget packages to the project:

- Microsoft.Azure.IoTHub.Serializer
- Microsoft.Azure.IoTHub.IoTHubClient
- Microsoft.Azure.IoTHub.MqttTransport


I have the following include statements:


```c
#include "iothubtransportmqtt.h"
#include "schemalib.h"
#include "iothub_client.h"
#include "serializer_devicetwin.h"
#include "schemaserializer.h"
#include "azure_c_shared_utility/threadapi.h"
#include "azure_c_shared_utility/platform.h"
#include <string.h>
```

With version 1.2.4 or earlier of the **Microsoft.Azure.IoTHub.IoTHubClient** package, the project builds. Any later version fails to build with:

```
Severity	Code	Description	Project	File	Line	Suppression State
Error (active)	E1696	cannot open source file "iothub_client_core_ll.h"	RMDevice	c:\temp\RMDevice\packages\Microsoft.Azure.IoTHub.IoTHubClient.1.2.5\build\native\include\iothub_client.h	24	
Error (active)	E1696	cannot open source file "iothub_client_core.h"	RMDevice	c:\temp\RMDevice\packages\Microsoft.Azure.IoTHub.IoTHubClient.1.2.5\build\native\include\iothub_client.h	25	

...

Error	C1083	Cannot open include file: 'iothub_client_core_ll.h': No such file or directory	RMDevice	c:\temp\rmdevice\packages\microsoft.azure.iothub.iothubclient.1.2.5\build\native\include\iothub_client.h	24	
```

comments:
[2018-07-31T21:37:26Z | jspaith | comment]
@kiranpradeep - thanks for calling this to our attention.

There's two issues I see here.  The first is that `map.h` ideally shouldn't be including `crt_abstractions.h` because this is getting us away from the API surface and getting us into internal utilities being exported out to the world.  `map.h` we need because of the (now deprecated) plumbing of the Map object to set properties.

We're going to look into whether we can decouple the header or not.  Also putting the `extern "C"` over everything is over-setting, so we'll look at scoping more, too.

[2018-08-02T18:08:27Z | BigPeteB | comment]
Leaving aside any distinction between private and public headers, the root cause is definitely that C++ headers are being included from inside `extern "C"` blocks.

This is definitely a cross-platform issue, not just something that affects MacOS.

Pull request #584 created.

@kiranpradeep - I don't have a Mac handy, but on Windows I was able to reproduce this problem and fix several offending header files. You could try pulling #584 (as well as updating the `uamqp` subrepo from Azure/azure-uamqp-c#254) and see if this fixes the problem for you as well.

[2018-08-13T16:48:01Z | jspaith | comment]
@BigPeteB's PR has addressed the C++ build issue (thank you!).  Closing this out as it impacted all platforms, but @kiranpradeep if you continue to hit issues please let us know.

[2018-08-13T16:48:06Z | az-iot-builder-01 | comment]
