**OS and version used: Windows 10 1809 64bit
SDK version used: release_2018_12_13**

We are using iothub_ll_client_x509_sample to send telemetry data to IoT Hub and noticed that this sample renews its connection after 48 minutes. Digging deeper, we realized that this is because of the following code in iothubtransport_mqtt_common.c.

https://github.com/Azure/azure-iot-sdk-c/blob/master/iothub_client/src/iothubtransport_mqtt_common.c#L2358

```
    size_t sas_token_expiry = IoTHubClient_Auth_Get_SasToken_Expiry(transport_data->authorization_module);
    if ((current_time - transport_data->mqtt_connect_time) / 1000 > (sas_token_expiry*SAS_REFRESH_MULTIPLIER))
    {
        /* Codes_SRS_IOTHUB_TRANSPORT_MQTT_COMMON_07_058: [ If the sas token has timed out IoTHubTransport_MQTT_Common_DoWork shall disconnect from the mqtt client and destroy the transport information and wait for reconnect. ] */
        DisconnectFromClient(transport_data);
```


(sas_token_expiry * SAS_REFRESH_MULTIPLIER) is 3600 sec*0.8 which turns out to be 2880 sec (48 min). This code seems to process the reconnection for SAS Token but we use x509 for authentication. 

We want to know whether this reconnection is expected behavior since our understanding is that connection renewal happens only when we use SAS token for authentication.

BTW, we get IOTHUB_CLIENT_CONNECTION_EXPIRED_SAS_TOKEN (after 48 min) when we check the connection status by using IoTHubClient_SetConnectionStatusCallback which seems odd.

