[2019-03-08T14:46:35Z | massand | comment]
@jitin17 - that is the expected SAS token refresh time for IoTHub, which IoTHub generally enforces and the SDK proactively refreshes, over the existing MQTT connection, every 45 minutes. You can register a connection callback via SetConnectionCallback and provide the traces for that alongside the IoTHub logs around the disconnect to be sure. You should see this combination of status / reason - IOTHUB_CLIENT_CONNECTION_UNAUTHENTICATED, IOTHUB_CLIENT_CONNECTION_EXPIRED_SAS_TOKEN.

[2019-03-12T10:21:27Z | jitin17 | comment]
@massand On reconnection, we see status/reason as IOTHUB_CLIENT_CONNECTION_UNAUTHENTICATED, IOTHUB_CLIENT_CONNECTION_EXPIRED_SAS_TOKEN.

As far I know, default SaS token expiry time is 3600 seconds(1 hour). So why is the reconnection happening after every ~45 mins?

[2019-03-14T00:31:13Z | massand | comment]
@jitin17 - it is a proactive SAS token refresh operation that causes the SAS token refresh to occur within the SDK. Otherwise, the application would need to recreate the IotHubDeviceClient handle again (since the active connection will no longer be valid after 3600 seconds) and deal with slightly longer IotHub connection downtime. 
@bikamani - Since you worked on different status codes being returned to the connection status callbacks for each transport, could you help add a section in this documentation around SAS token refresh scenarios and expected behavior? https://github.com/Azure/azure-iot-sdk-c/blob/6d51749b57152b54cbb06d51fe103d4b2b514247/doc/Iothub_sdk_options.md

[2019-04-03T01:50:38Z | ewertons | comment]
Documentation updated.
Please see ["Connection Authentication"](https://github.com/Azure/azure-iot-sdk-c/blob/master/doc/connection_and_messaging_reliability.md#connection-authentication) in our Connection and Messaging Reliability documentation.

