[2019-03-21T21:04:17Z | codetheweb | comment]
No problem.

That's some very elegant code, I like it. :)

I guess it's not necessarily an invalid packet, but adding parsing abilities for that would be a bit complex if we still want to support synchronous-style code.  For instance, a user might have a snippet that looks something like this:

```javascript
device.on('connect', () => {
  device.set(true).then(didWork => console.log);
  device.get().then(status => console.log);
});
```

(Not sure if we should be trying to support these kinds of patterns anyways. 🤷‍♂️)

Because they're not waiting for the first `device.set()` to finish before calling `device.get()`, TuyAPI would generate a packet similar to the one above.  But with the way TuyAPI currently works with Promise-style callbacks (adding a second listener to the 'data' event) just emitting 2 data events when receiving a packet with two responses wouldn't really work.

If we don't want to support the above pattern; I think it would be possible to fix this for the tests just by removing the `await this.get()` that's fired when a socket first connects to a device.  I'm still kind of torn about including it, it's a nice helper but does fire a call that's potentially unnecessary.

[2019-03-22T17:44:29Z | kueblc | comment]
What about using `sequence` from `TuyaFrame` to match requests with responses?

[2019-03-26T23:25:09Z | codetheweb | comment]
~~Forgot that packets included a sequence number, I'll look into it.~~

~~I guess not, I really thought they did.~~

Upon further investigation, it appears they don't unless you send a packet with a sequence number.  It may not understand the sequence number at all and it spits the prefix back out, as it doesn't appear to increment the number.

[2019-03-27T00:46:08Z | codetheweb | comment]
Hey @kueblc I [added support for sequence numbers](https://github.com/codetheweb/tuyapi/commit/c2bfe82d2ff5047a84a1fb5cf890965b9b0a66cc), it's a bit hacky at the moment but it'd be great if you could look it over / test it out.

It does simplify `.get()` and `.set()` so that's good.  The main issue I'm seeing is that sending a set payload to a device results in the device ignoring the sequence number and just returning 0.  Is that consistent with your experience?  My code currently accounts for it, but not very well.

