[2017-03-24T08:36:39Z | maggu2810 | comment]
@danchom Currently the ActionDTO uses `Map<String,String>` for `inputs`. Could this be related to.

[2017-03-24T11:59:52Z | danchom | comment]
I think the @jvivanco28 is mixing 
ActionDTO and ActionTypeDTO where the inputs field is define as List<Input> objects and they share same name but have different meaning. 

ActionDTO represents a action instance part of rule. Its inputs field is a map of String keys and String values. This map shows where the inputs of action instance are connected to. For example it should be: "
{
 "input1": "trigger1.output2",
 "input3": "actionX.output1"
} 
which means the input1 of this action instance is connected to output2 of trigger1 instance of the same rule and the input3 of this action instance is connected to output1 of actionX instance of the same rule.

The ActionTypeDTO represents ActionType which provides meta data related to all action instances of this type. The meta data defines names, types, default values, if they are required or not and etc. of configuration properties, inputs and outputs of action instances of this ActionType.  
The meta data related to inputs are used by the automation engine to verify connections(types of connected input and output), to check if all required connections are set and etc.

The rest command "PUT /rules/rule_1" uses action instance (where the inputs are in form of map of strings and define connections to other module), not ActionType definition. The action type definition has an input which has required field boolean,.

I don't see a bug here.

[2017-03-24T16:37:20Z | jvivanco28 | comment]
Yep. I think you're right. A bit of a mix up on our end differentiating between the Action and ActionType models.

Thanks for the quick and helpful feedback, guys. Very much appreciated!

