The XPATH transformation in a label seems to make some problems, when the xml contains a namespace which has to be resolved.
```
String http_Info_1 "Info [%s]" {http="<[http://campulverde.info/liveStreams.xml:60000:XPATH(/*[name()='liveStreams']/*[name()='stream']/*[name()='label'])]"}
String http_Info_2 "Info [XPATH(/*[name()='liveStreams']/*[name()='stream']/*[name()='label']):%s]" {http="<[http://campulverde.info/liveStreams.xml:60000:REGEX((.*))]"}
```

The first one is working, the second one does not, probably as the XPATH contains `[ ]`.

The `ItemRegistryImpl` does a somewhat "silent" migration of tags from items where they were previously persisted into the metadata storage.

However, this is conceptually flawed, as the tags remain persisted together with the item (as they were before #5431), i.e. if tags then are modified (especially removed) in the metadata, they will re-appear again after a restart. 

The following test-case illustrates this behavior:

```java
    @Test
    public void testReloadWithFormerlyPersistedTags() {
        prepareMetadata(ITEM_NAME, "foo");
        prepareItem(ITEM_NAME, "bar");

        // fake an item with previously persisted tag
        Object persistedObject = volatileStorageService.getStorage(Item.class.getName()).get(ITEM_NAME);
        ((PersistedItem) persistedObject).tags = Collections.singleton("bar");

        itemRegistry.removeTag(ITEM_NAME, "bar");
        assertEquals(1, itemRegistry.get(ITEM_NAME).getTags().size());

        // simulate a "reboot" by unregistering & re-registering the volatile storage service
        unregisterService(StorageService.class.getName());
        waitForAssert(() -> assertNull(itemRegistry.get(ITEM_NAME)));
        registerService(volatileStorageService, StorageService.class.getName());
        waitForAssert(() -> assertNotNull(itemRegistry.get(ITEM_NAME)));

        Item item = itemRegistry.get(ITEM_NAME);
        assertEquals(1, item.getTags().size()); // <--- THIS IS WERE IT FAILS
        assertEquals("foo", item.getTags().iterator().next());
    }
```

