[2017-05-16T12:17:31Z | exxamalte | comment]
I can confirm that this is reproducible.

Upload the following to a NodeMCU v2:
```
#include <Homie.h>

HomieNode lightNode1("my-super-awesome-node-idX", "switch");

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  Serial << endl << endl;
  Homie_setFirmware("bare-minimum", "1.0.0");
  Homie.setup();
}

void loop() {
  Homie.loop();
}
```

And the serial output looks like this:
```
 ets Jan  8 2013,rst cause:4, boot mode:(1,7)

wdt reset
```
After a manual reset the following appears in an endless loop:
```
ets Jan  8 2013,rst cause:2, boot mode:(3,6)

load 0x4010f000, len 1384, room 16
tail 8
chksum 0x2d
csum 0x2d
v3ffe8488
~ld
```

Further analysis leads to [`Limits.hpp`](https://github.com/marvinroger/homie-esp8266/blob/develop/src/Homie/Limits.hpp) where various string lengths are defined. In the above example with a long node ID, the limit is 24 characters. And there is actually a string length check in [`HomieNode.cpp`](https://github.com/marvinroger/homie-esp8266/blob/develop/src/HomieNode.cpp#L26), but for some reason the device just bails with the above message without outputting an error message.
There are several other maximum string lengths defined in `Limits.hpp` like type length or property length, but I cannot see the justification for the actual limits.

@marvinroger: Could you please have another look at this? Is this a bug, or just a known limitation? I am happy to help out with documenting these limits, but it would be good to understand why these were chosen. To be honest they look a bit arbitrary.
And of course it would be great if the device could handle too long strings properly with a helpful error message.

[2017-06-01T13:23:46Z | marvinroger | comment]
With a longer name, I can indeed reproduce. Fixed in https://github.com/marvinroger/homie-esp8266/commit/b3020a1e8647a7092078aa9b2d3778f38be56309

The limits are defined because Homie for ESP8266 avoids dynamic allocation, so it allocates resource beforehand, on the stack. To do that, we need to explicitely define "limits"

body:
Hi,

I'm using the latest develop commit (7dc2dd5) and when calling setProperty from a derived class on onReadyToOperate in the following conditions is causing MQTT Client to break, before sending any packet to the server.

Tested conditions
1.  Calling it twice from the same node
`void ANode::onReadyToOperate()
{
    	setProperty("enable").send("true");
    	setProperty("enable").send("true");
}`

2. Calling from two different nodes, but using the same property name
`void BNode::onReadyToOperate()
{
    	setProperty("enable").send("true");
}`
`void CNode::onReadyToOperate()
{
    	setProperty("enable").send("true");
}
`

Is this a limitation or a bug? Or am I using it wrong? Maybe is there some issue with the way I'm referencing the function?

If doing this:

```cpp
  mySetting.setDefaultValue(0).setValidator(
    function<bool (long)>([] (long value) {
      return value > 0;
    }));
```

The default value will not be validated against the validation function.  I *have not* tried doing this:

```cpp
  // cannot confirm if this has same behavior
  mySetting.setValidator(
    function<bool (long)>([] (long value) {
      return value > 0;
    })).setDefaultValue(0);
```

As to what should be done about this, I'm not sure; perhaps a warning or just documentation.  I could envision this being a valid use case.  For my use case, however, it's a programming error.

My Homie `HEAD` is bf7188e.

