[2017-09-24T14:12:21Z | user2684 | comment]
Hi, if referring to #198, I guess it may change something around the data structures used so I'd rather hold on to avoid you spend time on something which may change. But I'd love to see these changes applied just after , memory utilization is the first issue we have with Nodemanager right now honestly and any suggestion is more than welcome!

[2017-09-24T16:57:32Z | Akubi | comment]
Hi, yes and no.

In fact, in order to decrease the amount of used memory in a node with multiple child I tested a modified version of NodeManager.

The idea is to have only one message structure for outgoing messages in the system. In order to achieve that I changed some structures and some methods.

Mainly :

In sensor Class, I remove the _msg_service message end changed the _msg to a message pointer.

```
/***************************************
   Sensor: generic sensor class
*/
class Sensor {
…
  protected:
    MyMessage* _msg;
    //MyMessage _msg_service;
```

I then splitt the send function :
```
void _send(MyMessage & msg);
void _sendService(MyMessage & msg);
```



In NodeManager Class, I add 2 methods in order to use NodeManager capability to send messages from sensor Class. 


```
/***************************************
