body:
If you try to drive a remote Homematic IP plug via a CCU rule as below, the plug via IOS will switch ON immediately, but will wait around 10/20 sec to change state to OFF.
The sam instruction via WEB is working correctly with no delays.

```
Sitemap:
Switch item=IP_Plug_1

item:
Switch IP_Plug_1 <socket> (prese)
Switch IP_Plug_1_on         "Plug 1 ON"         {homematic="program=Plug_IP_1_ON"}
Switch IP_Plug_1_off                "Plug 1 OFF"        {homematic="program=Plug_IP_1_OFF"}

rule:
rule "Plug IP switch state" 
 when
 Item IP_Plug_1 changed
 then
 switch(IP_Plug_1.state) {
 case ON : {
 sendCommand(IP_Plug_1_on,ON)
 logInfo("IP Socket 1 ON", "Triggered CCU program to switch ON")
 }
 case OFF : {
 sendCommand(IP_Plug_1_off,ON)
 logInfo("IP Socket 1 OFF", "Triggered CCU program to switch OFF")
 }
 }
end
```

https://community.particle.io/t/connecting-particle-to-google-doc/23543/18
In this thread I couldn's see an issue with the code till I actually tested and realised that there must be something wrong with the rate limit of `Particle.publish()`.
Although we are allowed one publish per second, going close - but not over - that limit causes frequent cloud disconnect/reconnect glitches.

With this code on a Photon running 0.5.1 I get consistent reconnects every 60sec.

``` cpp
const uint32_t msPublish = 1000;

void setup() {
}

void loop() {
    static uint32_t ms;

    if (millis() - ms > msPublish)
    {
        ms = millis();
        Particle.publish("ScruffyTest", "Dummy!", PRIVATE);
    }
}
```

But when I increase `msPublish = 1100` the problem goes away - but even `1005` is too little (`1010` seems to be OK in most cases)

