## Bug Report

### Expected Behavior

You can call `Particle.keepAlive(30);` at any time and it should ping the server every 30 seconds. The system should keep track of the last TX/RX time and compare against the keepAlive interval (which if changed to a shorter setting may kick off a ping).

### Observed Behavior

No activity in the log output every 30 seconds as expected when keepAlive is set before we are connected to the Cloud.

### Steps to Reproduce

- Run the test app and observe the log output.
- Fails to work in THREADED or NON-THREADED mode
- Tested with system firmware 0.7.0-rc.4 and 0.8.0-rc.1

### Test App

```c
SerialLogHandler logHandler(LOG_LEVEL_ALL);

SYSTEM_THREAD(ENABLED);

void setup() {
    Particle.keepAlive(30); // send a ping every 30 seconds
}
```
### Workaround

Call keepAlive after we are connected to the Cloud.
```cpp
void loop() {
    static bool once = false;
    if (!once && Particle.connected()) {
        Particle.keepAlive(30); // send a ping every 30 seconds
        once = true;
    }
}
```

## Bug Report

### Expected Behavior

[subclassing LogHandler and implementing logMessage with code that generates logs](https://github.com/barakwei/ParticlePapertrail/blob/master/src/papertrail.cpp#L83) should behave sanely.

### Observed Behavior

The logging within the logger freezes the application thread while the LogHandler is trying to acquire a lock on the resource.

### Steps to Reproduce

Using system firmware 0.8.0-rc.2 and the following app

One fix tested that worked, recompiling user firmware locally changing `Mutex mutex_` to `RecursiveMutex mutex_` from [here](https://github.com/particle-iot/firmware/blob/develop/wiring/inc/spark_wiring_logging.h#L587) and also including a flag to prevent re-entry to the logMessage function (so logs generated from calls within the logMessage function don't cause a stack overflow due to recursion).

### Test App

```c
#include "Particle.h"

class UdpThing : LogHandler
{
private:

    UDP Udp;
    volatile bool active;

public:

    UdpThing(LogLevel level = LOG_LEVEL_INFO) :
            LogHandler(level),
            active(false) {
    }

    void begin()
    {
        Udp.begin(8888);
    }

    void addHandler() {
        LogManager::instance()->addHandler(this);
    }

    void logMessage(const char *msg, LogLevel level, const char *category, const LogAttributes &attr)
    {
        if (active) {
            return;
        }
        active = true;

        Log.info("logger: %s", msg);
        if (millis() > 15000) {
            Udp.beginPacket({8, 8, 8, 8}, 5000);
            Udp.write("testing ");
            Udp.endPacket();
            Log.info("application thread hangs here!!");
        }

        active = false;
    }

};

SerialLogHandler logHandler(LOG_LEVEL_ALL);  // all level over usb

UdpThing udpThing;

void setup()
{
    udpThing.begin();
    udpThing.addHandler();
}

void loop()
{
    Log.info("%lu ping", millis());
    delay(500);
}
```

### References

[issue 1](https://github.com/barakwei/ParticlePapertrail/issues/8)
[issue 2](https://github.com/barakwei/ParticlePapertrail/issues/7)
[issue 3](https://community.particle.io/t/papertrail-for-electron/37508)
[issue 4](https://community.particle.io/t/new-library-papertrail/28105/18?u=bdub)
[issue 5](https://community.particle.io/t/help-udp-send-in-loghandler-hangs-electron-w-0-6-1/30792)

```
void setup() 
{                                                                                                         
        Serial.begin(9600);                                                                               
        Serial1.end();                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             
        Particle.connect();
}                                                                                                         

/* This function loops forever --------------------------------------------*/                             
void loop()
{                                                                                                         

        delay(10 * 1000);
        Serial.printf("i am alive\r\n");                                                                  
        Serial.flush();                                                                                   
        static const pin_t pins_array[] = {D1, D3};                                               
        static const InterruptMode mode_array[] = {RISING, FALLING};                              

         System.sleep(pins_array, sizeof(pins_array)/sizeof(*pins_array), mode_array, sizeof(mode_array)/sizeof(*mode_array), 10);
}
```

On Gen3/Mesh devices it's currently virtually impossible to have two libraries using SPI work together without extra efforts.
Due to the fact that the mesh implementation of `SPI.begin(pin)` resets any previously set CS pin from OUTPUT to Hi-Z/INPUT when calling `SPI.begin(pin)` anew for another slave with its own CS pin. This prevents the library that had assigned it's CS pin before from useing `digitalWrite(pin, LOW)` in order to activate the slave.

A simple test sketch shows the difference between Gen2 and Gen3 devices in this respect
```
void setup() {
    Particle.function("SPITest", spiTest);
}

int spiTest(const char* pin) {
    int p = atoi(pin);
    if (p > 0) {
        SPI.begin(p);
    }
    return p;
}
```
When first calling `SPITest` for pin 7 (LED pin) the LED goes on. With a subsequent call for any other pin on e.g. an Argon the LED goes out indicating that the CS pin is not driven HIGH anymore, but on e.g. Photon the LED stays on.

In connection with that, reconsidering the choice to assign a pull-down (`NRF_GPIO_PIN_PULLDOWN`)  to MISO may be worth a thought. A saver bet may be `NRF_GPIO_PIN_NOPULL`.

This may also relate to this issue
https://github.com/particle-iot/device-os/issues/1290

