## Feature/Enhancement Request

How about an actual example in the docs of how you expect an user to start wifi and connect according to best practice (instead of SOS red lights) with 1.4.2?

How about you guys link your test case application code against the documentation? If it doesn't exist, how about writing some tests?

Assuming `BleUuid::shorted()` (which may be better called `::shortened()`, `::shorten()` or `::short()`) is meant to provide the 16bit UUID from a 128bit UUID it would need to *not* report the least siginificant two bytes of the 128 bits but actually the top 3rd and 4th (1st to 4th would be the 32bit) most siginificant.

According to the BT Core Specs a 128bit UUID is calculated as follows
```
128_bit_value = 16_bit_value * 2^96 + Bluetooth_Base_UUID
// or
128_bit_value = 32_bit_value * 2^96 + Bluetooth_Base_UUID
```
where `00000000-0000-1000-8000-00805F9B34FB` is the `Bluetooth_Base_UUID`.

Hence the 16 bit (short) UUID should be this part of any given 128bit UUID
```
0000xxxx-0000-1000-8000-00805F9B34FB
```
*BUT* `BleUuid::shorted()` always returns `0x34FB` which is *NOT* the 16bit short UUID but the least siginificant 16bit of the Base UUID - which is of no use for anything whatsoever - that's a fix value that could be hard coded just as well.

This may also impact `BlePeerDevice::getCharacteristicByUUID()` when the UUID is down-converted to 16bit the UUID will not be found as it won't be unique anymore.

A test code running on two (peripheral and central) Xenons 1.4.4 shows that the characteristics won't be found correctly 
